{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132854",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time it takes for a cab to traverse the avenue. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{3}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.53\\mu <X<1.47\\mu )=0.98.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.53\\mu <X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.98 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.98 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.49 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.5+0.49 \\\\ P(X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.99\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.47\\mu ) & =0.99 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.47\\mu }{3}) & =0.99 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.47\\mu }{3}) & =0.99 \\\\ \\frac{0.47\\mu }{3} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.99)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.99)≈2.3263.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.47\\mu }{3} & =2.3263 \\\\ 0.47\\mu  & =6.9789 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{6.9789}{0.47} \\\\  & ≈14.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
